The important division is between **source ownership** and
**entry activation**. A root file selects a role. Importing a public
declaration never runs an imported module's entry.

### Who owns each piece?

- `config.moth` supplies optional project and builder settings. It is not
  a module and has no runtime entry.
- `@entry.moth` owns one selected page's direct fragments, builder slots and
  runtime start. It publishes no API.
- `@package.moth` publishes a scoped support surface to eligible sibling
  branches and their descendants.
- `@mod.moth` publishes a normal module surface to its parent.
- Ordinary `.moth` files contain shared implementation inside their
  module. They are not implicitly public.
- `.mtf` files produce compile-time String content through the normal
  source-kind adapter. They do not read the entry's private bindings.

A directory can have any subset of the three root files. Each root keeps
its own declaration scope even when the files share one structural module.
`$export` belongs to the two facade roles and cannot expose entry work.

### Keep module dependencies deliberate

The example's `@components` support package is visible to the site root,
the `docs` branch and its child pages because of the scoped package's
position. It is not a universal global import. Other support packages in
the same scope cannot import it just because they are siblings.

A dependency clause selects names, not a runnable file. Organise components
under a facade when several pages need the same public surface. Keep private
markup helpers close to the package's implementation. Move a declaration
into a child module when that module has its own meaningful public API.

Do not use an old `+*.moth` root or invent page names such as
`@functions.moth`. Only `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and
`@entry.moth` establish root roles.

### Why the entry stays simple

Direct top-level `["[...]"]` fragments must fold at compilation. The compiler
preserves their source order and the builder assembles them into the final
document. This avoids constructing static content during page startup.

A direct `["~[...]"]` fragment opts into runtime output. The page's entry
runs once when selected. Runtime fragments are published after successful
startup, so a failed start cannot publish half of that invocation's dynamic
fragments. Earlier static fragments remain.

Use `#=` when a folded template needs a name, a field or an importable value.
That binding remains data: it does not emit a page merely by being declared.

### HTML belongs to the builder

The HTML builder owns directory routes, metadata destinations, resource
output and URL rendering. Only a **direct** entry-root
`["[$in title:...]"]` or other registered builder contribution reaches it.
An ordinary nested `$in` targets its immediate template receiver.

Resources remain structural Strings until the consuming output has a URL
context. A shared component can carry a stylesheet reference without
guessing the URL for each page. Reusing that same resource origin on several
pages does not require duplicating the resource file.

See @../../project-structure (Project structure),
@../../packages (Packages) and @../../constants (Constants) for the
precise source and builder contracts.
